## Editing inside a nest

A nest is a group of clips collapsed into one clip (**Clip ▸ Nest**, ⌘G;
see [Editing](editing.md)). An agent can edit inside one two ways.

**Open it, as you would.** `set_scope` is double-clicking a nest clip, or
**Clip ▸ Open Nest** (⌘↓). Give it the `nest` id or the `clip` that shows
it, never both:

- `clip` is the precise choice, since two clips can show one nest and each
  measures the times inside from its own start. A clip that shows no nest is
  refused.
- `nest` opens only a nest that a clip on the timeline shows. Where two
  clips show it, the earliest is entered (ties go to the lower id). For a
  nest the timeline does not reach, use an op's `scope` instead (below).

While a nest is open:

- `get_project` reports the nest as if it were the project: its own tracks
  and clips, its frozen size as the canvas, and times from its own zero. It
  also reports `scope`, `scope_clip` and `scope_path` (the trail of nests
  from the timeline). At the timeline, `scope` is `null`.
- `render_frame` draws the nest at its own size.
- Every op lands inside the nest, unless it says otherwise.

Call `set_scope` with no arguments to come back out (**Clip ▸ Close Nest**,
⌘↑).

**Or name it on one call.** Every op tool also takes `scope`, a nest id.
With it, that one call's clip, track and action ids are looked up inside the
nest, and its times are the nest's own. It works from wherever the window
is looking and outranks whatever nest is open. Media ids are unaffected: a
nest shares the document's media. The answer echoes the `scope` it landed in.

**Tools that are not ops follow the open nest.** `detect_silence`,
`normalize_clip`, `sync_audio`, `split_at_shots`, `analyze_cutout`, `transcribe`,
`import_captions`, `export_captions`, `relay_captions` and `record_screen` take no `scope`
argument. They work in
whatever nest `set_scope` has open (`record_screen` places its clip there),
so open the nest first to measure or place inside it. `detect_silence`
names, under `protected_nests`, the nest clips a quiet run crossed and left
uncut; open one with `set_scope` and call again to look for dead air inside it. The
`ripple_delete_ranges` call that acts on `detect_silence`'s answer takes
`scope` like any op.

### What does not work inside a nest

- Eleven op tools have no meaning inside a nest and refuse a `scope` by name:
  `add_marker`, `set_marker`, `remove_marker`, `set_canvas`, `add_media`,
  `relink_media`, `remove_media`, `set_media_color`, `set_media_speech`,
  `set_media_cursor` and `add_adjustment_clip`.
- When the nest is only *open* (from `set_scope`) rather than named, most of
  those simply apply to the whole document instead, and the answer lists them
  under `document_ops`. The two that carry a time — `add_marker`, and
  `set_marker` with a `time` — are refused while a nest is open, whatever
  time you send, because a marker is at project time. Leave the nest first
  (`set_scope` with no arguments), then send the marker's time in project
  ticks.
- An edit can be valid inside the nest and still be refused for the
  document's sake. The clip and track limits and the 8-deep nesting limit
  count every container at once.

## A typical session

1. `get_project` — read the ids, the canvas and the playhead.
2. `import_media {path}` — answers the new media id and what the file holds.
3. `add_clip {track, media, at}` — place it.
4. Edit with the op tools.
5. `render_frame {t}` — look at the result. Use it before and after edits.
6. `save_project {path}` — answers once the package is really on disk.
7. `export {preset, path}`, then `export_status {export_id}` until it is done.

## Checking the picture: render_frame

`render_frame` renders one frame of the finished timeline through the same
composition and compositor an export uses, so it shows what the file will
contain at that moment. The picture comes back both as an image the model can
see and as a file on disk, whose path the answer's text names.

| Argument | What it does | Default |
|---|---|---|
| `t` | The moment to render, in ticks. | The playhead |
| `max_width` | Caps the width in pixels (16–8192), aspect kept, never upscaled. | The canvas size |
| `path` | Where to write the file. The folder must exist. | A temporary file |
| `format` | `png`, `jpeg` or `heic`. Wins over the path's extension, which is replaced. PNG and HEIC keep the canvas's transparency; JPEG flattens it onto black. | From the path, else PNG |
| `burn_in_timecode` | Draws the project timecode over the picture. | Off |
| `ungraded` | Draws every clip as if it were not graded (**View ▸ Show Ungraded**'s picture). | Off |
| `sdr_version` | In an HDR project, draws its SDR version (**View ▸ Show SDR Version**'s picture). | Off |
| `selection` | An adjustment clip's id: draws what its qualifier selects (**View ▸ Show Selection**'s picture). | None |
| `waveform`, `parade`, `vectorscope`, `histogram` | Also measures that scope. The text gives the clipping or neutral share, and a JSON file beside the image holds the counts. | Off |
| `vectorscope_zoom` | 1, 2 or 4, with `vectorscope`. | 1 |

It is the same writer as **File ▸ Save Frame…**, so a still saved from the
menu and one written here are identical. When a graphic in the frame failed
or warned, the answer names it, and a failed graphic draws its error card in
the picture. A clip with Remove Background (`set_video` `cutout`) waits up to
30 seconds for the masks the frame needs, which Frame Job makes as soon as the
cutout is set; past that the clip is drawn whole and a sentence before the last
says so: "Clip *n* is drawn uncut: its person masks were not ready within
30 s." (for a picture with subject `foreground`, "its foreground masks").

In an HDR (HLG) project, a PNG is 16-bit and a HEIC 10-bit, both labelled
BT.2100 HLG. A JPEG, and any frame asked for with `sdr_version`, is the
project's SDR version, labelled BT.709. The scopes then read the HLG signal,
and their JSON adds `signal: "hlg"` and, for the waveform, parade and
histogram, a `scale`. Scopes are described in
[Colour grading](color-grading.md), HDR frames in [HDR and colour management](hdr-and-color.md).

## Exporting: start, then poll
## Reading the project: get_project

`get_project` answers the whole project as JSON, the same document every op
edits:

- **`project`** — the canvas (width, height, fps as `[num, den]`, background,
  `fit_new_clips`, `timecode_frames`, dynamic range and blending), `media`,
  `tracks`, `clips`, `markers`, `nests` (each with its own tracks and clips)
  and `next_id`.
- **The app's own state**, which is not saved in the project: `playhead`,
  `selection`, and `in_out_range` when one is set.
- `path`, `name`, `unsaved_changes` and `duration`.
- The `scope` report (which nest is open, if any).
- `missing_media` when files have moved (`relink_media` points one at its
  new location).
- `graphics` — every module in the package's `graphics/` folder, whether it
  compiles, and the clips drawn by it.

Read it first: every other tool names things by the ids it reports.

## The tools
use crate::outcome::Outcome;

/// Reads the envelope's `scope` out of the value `edit::parse` removed.
///
/// `None` for absent and for an explicit `null` — the envelope's standing
/// rule that the two mean the same thing — and an `Err` naming the value
/// for anything that is not a nest id, which is any u32. A negative number,
/// a fraction, a numeric STRING and a bool all land there: a scope decides
/// which document an edit lands in, so guessing at one is the last thing to
/// do.
pub(crate) fn read(value: Option<&Value>) -> Result<Option<u32>, String> {
    match value {
        None | Some(Value::Null) => Ok(None),
        Some(found) => found
            .as_u64()
            .and_then(|id| u32::try_from(id).ok())
            .map(Some)
            .ok_or_else(|| format!("scope must be a nest id, not {}", describe(found))),
    }
}

/// The ops with no meaning inside a nest, by name, or `None` for the
/// fifty-six that work in there unchanged.
///
/// Exhaustive rather than a short list with a `_` arm, and that is the
/// point of writing it out: an op added by a later phase does not compile
/// until somebody has decided which side of this line it falls on. The
/// compiler is the reviewer.
fn refused_inside(op: &Op) -> Option<&'static str> {
    match op {
        // A nest has no `markers[]` (spec §5.2). The timeline's markers are
        // PROJECT time and chapter points on the exported file; a marker
        // measured from a nest's own zero would be neither.
        Op::AddMarker { .. } => Some("add_marker"),
        Op::SetMarker { .. } => Some("set_marker"),
        Op::RemoveMarker { .. } => Some("remove_marker"),

        // Half of a canvas is not a nest's to have — the rate, the
        // background and the two switches are the document's — and the half
        // that is, its size, is FROZEN at the size the parent had when the
        // nest was made, so that a later `set_canvas` cannot silently
        // re-lay-out content nobody touched ([`Nest`]). Honouring that here
        // would be `set_canvas` reaching through a scope to do the one
        // thing the freeze exists to stop.
        Op::SetCanvas { .. } => Some("set_canvas"),

        // A nest references the parent's `media[]` in the parent's one id
        // space (spec §5.2). So a media edit inside a scope is a media edit
        // on the document wearing a scope — the same act, spelled twice —
        // and `remove_media` is worse than redundant: it would strip the
        // clips that show the file from the view's containers and leave
        // every TIMELINE clip that shows it dangling, since those are not
        // in the view to be found.
        Op::AddMedia { .. } => Some("add_media"),
        Op::RelinkMedia { .. } => Some("relink_media"),
        Op::RemoveMedia { .. } => Some("remove_media"),
        Op::SetMediaColor { .. } => Some("set_media_color"),
        Op::SetMediaSpeech { .. } => Some("set_media_speech"),
        Op::SetMediaCursor { .. } => Some("set_media_cursor"),

        // An adjustment clip grades what is behind it at the top level only
        // (spec §11 GR-6): `check_clips` refuses one inside a nest, so the
        // op is refused here before a view is built.
        Op::AddAdjustmentClip { .. } => Some("add_adjustment_clip"),

        // A range kept as a whole project is the DOCUMENT's timeline, edited
        // only on a derived document (spec §11 CJ-50); a nest has none.
        Op::KeepRange { .. } => Some("keep_range"),

        // A track's dynamics run on the TIMELINE's per-track sub-mixes (spec
        // §11 AU-150): a nest's whole mix passes through the track holding
        // its nest clip, which is the bus, and a nest's own tracks have no
        // sub-mix of their own to compress. The rest of `set_track` means
        // inside a nest what it means outside (below).
        Op::SetTrack {
            compressor,
            limiter,
            ..
        } if compressor.is_some() || limiter.is_some() => Some("a track's compressor or limiter"),

        // ---- everything else ------------------------------------------
    ///
    /// Internal rather than private for one reason and no other: three other
    /// files build tools with it or with `opTool`, and `private` on an
    /// extension member reaches only its own file.
    func tool(
        _ name: String, _ description: String,
        _ properties: [String: Any], required: [String] = []
    ) -> [String: Any] {
        var schema = properties
        schema["project"] = Self.projectProperty
        // Road map 2's phase 9: every §6 OP also takes the envelope's
        // `scope`, and it is injected HERE for the reason `project` is —
        // one edit site instead of sixty-four, and the op somebody adds in
        // phase 10 cannot arrive with a tool that silently lacks it.
        //
        // Op-ness is the CORE's answer (`Self.opNames`, read through
        // `rc_op_names`) and never a list kept in this file: the startup
        // parity check already reads that list to insist every op has a
        // tool, and a second spelling of it is exactly the drift this
        // file's contract exists to prevent. It also draws the line
        // correctly without an exception list — `detect_silence` and
        // `normalize_clip` are composites rather than ops, and
        // `AgentServer+Scope.swift` argues why neither takes a scope.
        //
        // It OVERWRITES a `scope` a caller wrote into `properties`, exactly
        // as `project` does. Nothing does today, and the near miss is worth
        // naming: `detect_silence` answers a RESULT key called `scope` —
        // which set of clips it measured — and takes no argument by that
        // name, so the two spellings never meet in one schema.
        if Self.opNames.contains(name) { schema["scope"] = Self.scopeProperty }
        return [
            "name": name,
            "description": description,
            "inputSchema": [
                "type": "object", "properties": schema, "required": required,
            ] as [String: Any],
        ]
    }

    /// One OP tool: `tool` above, with every argument's STRUCTURE printed
    /// from the core's published table instead of written here (road map
            + "and a nest clip's source.project names the one it shows. With it, every id in "
            + "this call — clips, tracks, actions — is looked up in THAT nest's own tracks "
            + "and clips instead of the timeline's, and every time is the nest's own, "
            + "measured from its zero (a nest has no speed, so the whole mapping is t minus "
            + "the nest clip's start); media ids are unaffected, because a nest shares the "
            + "document's media. The op itself is unchanged and one call is still one undo "
            + "step. Omit it to edit whatever the app has OPEN — the timeline, or the nest "
            + "set_scope entered, exactly as an edit made by hand goes where the window is "
            + "looking — so this argument is how you act on a container you are not looking "
            + "at, and get_project always says which one you are in. Eight ops have no "
            + "meaning inside a nest and refuse by name when you ask for one: add_marker, "
            + "set_marker, remove_marker, set_canvas, add_media, relink_media, remove_media, "
            + "add_adjustment_clip. The first seven behave DIFFERENTLY when the scope is inherited from set_scope "
            + "rather than asked for here, and the result says which: five of them, plus "
            + "set_marker's name / chapter / color and remove_marker, simply land on the "
            + "DOCUMENT and are named under document_ops. The two that carry a TIME — "
            + "add_marker, and set_marker with a time — are refused instead, because a "
            + "marker is at project time while the playhead inside a nest is not, so the "
            + "tick you would send means the wrong frame. Leave the nest (set_scope with "
            + "no arguments) or pass the tick in project time. "
            + "And read the result rather than assuming it landed: an edit can be perfectly "
            + "valid inside the nest and still be refused for the whole DOCUMENT's sake, "
            + "since the clip and track caps and the 8-deep nesting limit are counted across "
            + "every container at once.",
    ]

    /// The sentence every time argument carries. Ticks are the model's own
    /// unit (spec §5.1) and the only one the ops take.
    private static let ticksNote =
        "in TICKS — 600000 per second, so 1 s = 600000 and one 30 fps frame = 20000. "
        + "Rounded to the project's frame grid."
    /// (`EditorViewController+AddClips.swift`). Stamping the scope on both
    /// makes the whole drop an error; stamping it on neither drops the clip on
    /// the timeline. So `ProjectDocument` sends a document-level op UNSCOPED
    /// and everything else scoped, which is also why File ▸ Add Media…, a
    /// Relink and a Remove go on working with a nest open instead of being
    /// greyed out.
    ///
    /// Nothing here is a second opinion about what those ops MEAN: the core
    /// refuses a scoped one by name either way, and an agent that asks for one
    /// with a `scope` still gets that refusal, because `scoped(_:)` above is
    /// the plain stamp and this list is consulted only by the app's own edit
    /// paths.
    static let documentLevelNames: Set<String> = [
        "add_marker", "set_marker", "remove_marker", "set_canvas", "add_media", "relink_media",
        "remove_media", "set_media_color", "set_media_speech", "set_media_cursor", "keep_range",
    ]

    /// True for one of the eleven above — an edit about the document rather
    /// than about whatever container the timeline is looking at.
    var isDocumentLevel: Bool { Self.documentLevelNames.contains(name) }
}

// MARK: - Op factories: clips

extension ReelOp {
    /// Whole media as one clip at `at`; Fit scale when the canvas says so.
    static func addClip(track: UInt32, media: UInt32, at: Ticks) -> ReelOp {
        var a = OpArgs()
        a.put("track", track)
        a.put("media", media)
        a.put("at", at)
        return ReelOp(name: "add_clip", args: a.values)
    }

    /// A text clip; `duration` defaults to 5 s and `text` to the Title
    /// preset in the core.
     592 app/Sources/AgentServer+Scope.swift
     334 app/Sources/AgentServer+Render.swift
     439 app/Sources/AgentCatalog+Render.swift
     123 app/Sources/AgentServer+Scopes.swift
    1488 total

